      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "When we take a total mean, we can multiply out each thing we're adding (or subtracting) to get the total. We have 30 students and 1 point each, and $72 \\%$. When we have 6 more students with 78 each, we have 78 and a large number of 6s. We can subtract the old class mean times 30 from its total mean times 30:\n\n$30 \\cdot 72 \\%=216.$\nThen we want to find out the total of the six students we added. We know that we added 6 of them and 78 points, so we have:\n$\\frac{6 \\cdot 78}{6}=78.$\nFinally, we want to add both of these quantities separately and then add them together, because they're not made up of the same group:\n\n$78-216=-138.$\nSubtracting that value from our total, we get:\n\n$216-138=78.$\nOur total score is 78 percent, and there are now 36 students taking the test. We can now take a total mean of the class as follows:\n\n$\\frac{78}{36}=22$.\nAnd zero percent off $22$ percent means $22 \\%$.\n\nThe class mean is $\\boxed{22 \\%}$. The answer is 22",
